WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.880
- A lot of folks think that a librarian

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:04.580
is just someone who checks books
in and out of a library for you,

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:05.560
but that's not true.

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:09.780
"Librarian" is a highly skilled profession
that requires a lot of specialised training.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:12.000
It's about helping people research
and access knowledge,

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.680
in whatever way makes sense.

00:00:13.686 --> 00:00:18.140
Through the 20th century, that knowledge
was held in books and on microfilm tapes

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:21.193
stored in central buildings in each town,
like this one,

00:00:21.193 --> 00:00:22.060
as a public good,

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:24.670
which is actually almost
miraculous when you think about it.

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:28.160
The modern public library
is a revolutionary, wonderful idea.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:30.900
Knowledge and information
should be available to everyone,

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:33.810
not just the rich and not just the clever.

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:36.400
In the 21st century, that's still true.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:38.210
A lot of that information
might be online now

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:39.520
and available through our phones,

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.960
but in-depth research,
searching out truth from fiction,

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:46.035
shouldn't be just for people
who are able to understand

00:00:46.035 --> 00:00:49.340
complex academic citations
and referencing.

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:51.836
If you don't know how
to look something up,

00:00:51.836 --> 00:00:54.490
if you don't even know really
what you're looking for,

00:00:54.490 --> 00:00:55.640
if you don't have the words,

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:58.090
the librarian's job
is to help you find out.

00:00:58.090 --> 00:01:00.380
In an ideal world that's what
librarians would be doing,

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:02.714
not pushing a cart and
moving books around by hand.

00:01:02.714 --> 00:01:05.971
That's why here at Oodi, the
new Helsinki Central Library,

00:01:05.971 --> 00:01:09.670
those books are being moved by a robot.

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:12.100
- This Oodi building was built last year,

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:13.800
so it's a pretty new building

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:17.660
and that's why we have
a lot of new technology

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:19.710
and new services in here.

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:23.420
It has been said that it's
like a living room for citizens.

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:28.910
So, for example, in the first floor,
we have a movie theatre.

00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:31.510
Then, in the second floor,
we have things to do.

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:36.170
For example, 3D printers and laser cutters

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:39.600
and music studios, and even a kitchen.

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:42.110
You can play computer games.

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:45.970
We have VR classes there,
and gaming computers.

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:48.090
Oodi isn't that traditional library,

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:50.300
so in the third floor,
where we have our books,

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:52.410
it's not that silent space.

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:54.470
People can talk there freely.

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:58.370
And so, we have a few
classrooms for silent reading.

00:01:58.370 --> 00:02:00.900
In normal big libraries,

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:03.610
there's usually some
kind of returning machine

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:06.200
that sorts the books to the right places.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:07.940
We have also that here.

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:10.710
The customers can return
books from the first floor,

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:15.980
and they will come,
using the conveyor, to this basement

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:19.800
and they will be sorted
by genre and language.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:21.050
That's pretty basic stuff,

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:24.640
but we are in progress
of further automating it.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:27.650
So, we have a couple of robotic arms,

00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:30.340
and then we have three mobile robots.

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:33.700
Those mobile robots
will pick up these boxes

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:37.621
and they will be transferred
by them to the third floor,

00:02:37.621 --> 00:02:40.930
where staff can shelve the books.

00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:45.520
Small part of books will be
shelved by our robotic arm

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:48.410
that will the put the books in shelves.

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:50.570
And when the shelf is full,

00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:54.000
it will be transferred
by those mobile robots.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:58.101
Then we have some plans
to use mobile robots

00:02:58.101 --> 00:03:02.722
in nights to scan the books with
RFID antennas,

00:03:02.722 --> 00:03:05.610
so we know if there's any books
in wrong places.

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:07.540
Those mobile robots that we are using

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:11.660
are planned so that they can
avoid collisions with people.

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:14.900
If someone gets in front of our robot

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:17.320
it will calculate new routes

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:21.900
and find another way to
get to the right position.

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:24.970
At least at this point, there
hasn't been any accidents

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:27.760
or things like that, so
the robots and humans

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:29.970
have been getting together very well.

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:32.740
And people are quite curious about them

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:37.360
and why we have them here,
and what they can do.

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:39.770
- This library gets a lot
of press for its robots.

00:03:39.770 --> 00:03:41.950
There was even a design
prototype with googly eyes

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:43.600
that guided people to
the shelf they wanted.

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:46.520
But most people can read a
map or follow directions.

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:47.970
The real job of the robots here

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:50.600
is the same as robots everywhere:
automation.

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:54.465
Don't waste the librarians' time with
tasks that a computer can now do.

00:03:54.465 --> 00:03:58.130
If you think public libraries are just about
collections of books then, yeah,

00:03:58.130 --> 00:03:59.770
of course they seem outdated.

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:03.520
Public libraries in the 21st century
can be where you go to borrow a book,

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:04.510
that's not going to stop,

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:07.570
but libraries were never just about books.

00:04:07.570 --> 00:04:10.890
They're about information.
Free at the point of use, no price tag.

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:13.350
They're about collaboration
and communication.

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:16.753
And about making sure that
everyone can find truth.

00:04:16.753 --> 00:04:18.810
Most libraries don't have robots,

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:20.676
but every library does have librarians.

00:04:20.676 --> 00:04:22.693
And they're what's important.

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:26.283
Thanks so much to all the team at Oodi
for showing me around.

00:04:26.283 --> 00:04:28.730
Find out more about them
by clicking the link in the description.

